+# BLOCKED: 002_unique_email.sql on the production snapshot
+
+## What is blocked
+
+`migrations/002_unique_email.sql` makes `users.email` `NOT NULL` and
+case-insensitively unique. That migration can be written and works on a fresh
+database, but it cannot be applied to the production snapshot without violating
+the requirement that the migration "must not modify or delete any customer rows".
+
+## Why
+
+`fixtures/prod_snapshot.sql` already contains data that violates both new
+constraints:
+
+- **Missing email** — `id = 17` (`Goran Okafor`) has `email IS NULL`, so it
+  cannot satisfy `NOT NULL`.
+- **Case-insensitive duplicates** — ids `1`, `4`, and `23` all collapse to
+  `ana.silva@example.com` under `lower(email)`:
+  - id 1: `ana.silva@example.com`
+  - id 4: `Ana.Silva@example.com`
+  - id 23: `ana.silva@example.com` (also an exact duplicate of id 1)
+
+SQLite cannot add a `NOT NULL` constraint to an existing column, so the
+migration rebuilds `users` and creates a unique index on `lower(email)`. Both
+steps validate existing rows, which means they fail while the violating rows
+above are present.
+
+The only ways to make the constraints hold are to edit the rows (choose a value
+for id 17, rewrite the casing of id 4) or delete rows (ids 4 and 23). Both are
+explicitly forbidden: Support owns that data, and this migration must leave
+every row untouched. There is no SQLite schema construct that enforces
+`NOT NULL` and case-insensitive uniqueness over existing data without either
+changing the offending rows or letting them remain in violation. Enforcing the
+rules with triggers alone would only constrain future writes and would leave
+the table's existing data non-compliant, so it is not a real solution.
+
+## What the migration does instead
+
+`002_unique_email.sql` is implemented for compliant data, including the fresh
+database case. It builds and validates a replacement table (including the unique
+index) before dropping the original, then commits atomically. On the production
+snapshot it aborts without committing any change to `users`, rather than
+silently mutating or deleting customer rows.
+
+## What is needed to unblock
+
+Support must reconcile the production data first:
+
+1. Provide a real email address for user id 17 (or otherwise resolve the NULL).
+2. Decide which of ids 1, 4, and 23 is canonical and correct/remove the others
+   according to Support's data policy.
+
+Once the snapshot is clean, this migration can be applied unchanged.
